<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.4.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01802</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.4.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>ccf65979-8221-4a4d-a203-05736c2cddae</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
<section><exercise id="_4b80f75e-83fa-47fa-908b-5f41956b3084" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Use the following to answer questions 1 – 7.</para><para>The table shows values of the expressions \(10x^2\) and \(2^x\).</para>
      <table class="os-raise-skinnytable">
        <tgroup cols="3"><tbody><row>
            <entry><emphasis>\(x\)</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>\(10x^2\)</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>\(2^x\)</emphasis></entry></row>
        <row>
            <entry>\(1\)</entry>
            <entry>\(10\)</entry>
            <entry>\(2\)</entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>\(2\)</entry>
            <entry>\(40\)</entry>
            <entry>\(4\)</entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>\(3\)</entry>
            <entry>\(90\)</entry>
            <entry>\(8\)</entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>\(4\)</entry>
            <entry>\(160\)</entry>
            <entry>\(16\)</entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>\(8\)</entry>
            <entry> </entry>
            <entry> </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>\(10\)</entry>
            <entry> </entry>
            <entry> </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>\(12\)</entry>
            <entry> </entry>
            <entry> </entry>
          </row>
        </tbody></tgroup></table>
      
      <para>Complete the statement.</para>
        
        <para>When the value of \(x\) increases by \(1\), the expression \(10x^2\):</para>
        <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>Grows by increasing amounts but by decreasing growth factors.</para><para>Changes exponentially. It doubles.</para><para>Grows by decreasing amounts but by increasing growth factors.</para><para>Changes logarithmically. It has decreased by one-half.</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! When the value of \(x\) increases by \(1\), the expression \(10x^2\) grows by increasing amounts but
        by decreasing growth factors.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.4.2.</para><para>The correct answer is: When the value of \(x\) increases by \(1\), the expression \(10x^2\) grows by increasing
        amounts but by decreasing growth factors.

     </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_8faaff31-6900-41de-8a2d-b80d6410eef6" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>When the value of \(x\) increases by \(1\), the expression \(2^x\):</para>
        <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>Grows by increasing amounts but by decreasing growth factors.</para><para>Changes exponentially. It doubles.</para><para>Grows by decreasing amounts but by increasing growth factors.</para><para>Changes logarithmically. It has decreased by one-half.</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! When the value of \(x\) increases by \(1\), the expression \(2^x\) changes exponentially. It doubles.
     </para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.4.2.</para><para>The correct answer is: When the value of \(x\) increases by \(1\), the expression \(2^x\) changes
        exponentially. It doubles.
     </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_8e5a4f31-c589-4967-9720-c254217f286b" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Will the value of \(10x^2\) be greater than or less than \(2^x\) when \(x\) is \(8\)?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! The value of \(10x^2\) will be greater than \(2^x\) when \(x\) is \(8\).
     </para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.4.2 and 7.4.3.</para><para>The correct answer is: The value of \(10x^2\) will be greater than \(2^x\) when \(x\) is \(8\).

     </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_5f718188-4505-4e71-963d-58f11b82edeb" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Will the value of \(2^x\) be greater than or less than \(10x^2\) when \(x\) is \(10\)?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! The value of \(2^x\) will be greater than \(10x^2\) when \(x\) is \(10\).</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.4.2 and 7.4.3.</para><para>The correct answer is: The value of \(2^x\) will be greater than \(10x^2\) when \(x\) is \(10\).</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="f994a359-6d8c-4c0e-a52a-1881e76fddee" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Will the value of \(10x^2\) be greater than or less than \(2^x\) when \(x\) is \(12\)?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! The value of \(10x^2\) will be less than \(2^x\) when \(x\) is \(12\).</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.4.2 and 7.4.3.</para><para>The correct answer is: The value of \(10x^2\) will be less than \(2^x\) when \(x\) is \(12\).
     </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="d5b7d872-1b85-4b80-b66c-70b4f87e9d07" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Determine the following values of \(10x^2\).</para>
        
        <para>What is the value of \(10x^2\) when \(x\) is \(8\)?</para>
        </problem><solution><para>Correct! When \(x=8\), the value of \(10x^2\) is \(640\).</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.4.2.</para><para>The correct answer is: When \(x=8\), the value of \(10x^2\) is \(640\).</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_705515a5-0d88-4824-b33d-09be5291b64f" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>What is the value of \(10x^2\) when \(x\) is \(10\)?</para>
        </problem><solution><para>Correct! When \(x=10\), the value of \(10x^2\) is \(1000\).</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.4.2.</para><para>The correct answer is: When \(x=10\), the value of \(10x^2\) is \(1000\).</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="a4152af5-599e-42fa-9475-7c5b64709d38" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>What is the value of \(10x^2\) when \(x\) is \(12\)?</para>
        </problem><solution><para>Correct! When \(x=12\), the value of \(10x^2\) is \(1440\).</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.4.2.</para><para>The correct answer is: When \(x=12\), the value of \(10x^2\) is \(1440\).</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_37027873-e96f-4d70-8e77-f20e2bbd7858" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Determine the following values of \(2^x\).
       </para>
        
        <para>What is the value of \(2^x\) when \(x\) is \(8\)?</para>
        </problem><solution><para>Correct! When \(x=8\), the value of \(2^x\) is \(256\).</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.4.2.</para><para>The correct answer is: When \(x=8\), the value of \(2^x\) is \(256\).</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="d5128dc7-5a90-47d9-833d-59629ceb89e7" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>What is the value of \(2^x\) when \(x\) is \(10\)?</para>
        </problem><solution><para>Correct! When \(x=10\), the value of \(2^x\) is \(1024\).</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.4.2.</para><para>The correct answer is: When \(x=10\), the value of \(2^x\) is \(1024\).</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_7cea577a-7c41-4637-bb49-e502c9900882" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>What is the value of \(2^x\) when \(x\) is \(12\)?</para>
        </problem><solution><para>Correct! When \(x=12\), the value of \(2^x\) is \(4096\).</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.4.2.</para><para>The correct answer is: When \(x=12\), the value of \(2^x\) is \(4096\).</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_5f1f6d4a-2063-423d-b50d-ef721491cfdc" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Choose the best statement that describes how the values of the exponential expression \(2^x\) and the
          quadratic expression \(10x^2\) change as \(x\) becomes greater and greater.</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>The value of the exponential expression grows much more quickly as \(x\) increases.</para><para>The value of the quadratic expression grows much more quickly as \(x\) increases.</para><para>They both grow at the same rate.</para><para>There is no change in the values of the expressions when \(x\) increases.</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! The value of the exponential expression grows much more quickly as \(x\) increases.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.4.2 and 7.4.3.</para><para>The correct answer is: The value of the exponential expression grows much more quickly as \(x\) increases.</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_1ade4671-a4e8-4dfc-8bd1-b829a50b4e25" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Answer the following questions about functions \(f(x)\) and \(g(x)\).</para>
        
        <para>What type of expression is \(f(x)=1.5^x\)?</para>
        </problem><solution><para>Correct! \(f(x)=1.5^x\) is an exponential expression.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.4.2.</para><para>The correct answer is: \(f(x)=1.5^x\) is an exponential expression.</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_7e9ff011-14bb-48df-a9e1-c15379946ea4" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>What type of expression is \(g(x)=500x^2+345x\)?</para>
        </problem><solution><para>Correct! \(g(x)=500x^2+345x\) is a quadratic expression.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.4.2.</para><para>The correct answer is: \(g(x)=500x^2+345x\) is a quadratic expression.</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_3161a9b3-dd78-407a-ba76-a63b3e7e778e" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>The values of which function will eventually be greater for larger and larger values of \(x\)?</para>
        <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>Function \(f(x)\)</para><para>Function \(g(x)\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! The exponential function, \(f\), will eventually be greater for larger and larger values of \(x\).</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.4.2 and 7.4.3.</para><para>The correct answer is: The exponential function, \(f\), will eventually be greater for larger and larger values
        of \(x\).</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="f710b5e0-1775-4c25-8da8-e04c35ea4aff" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>The table shows the values of \(4^x\) and \(100x^2\) for some values of \(x\).
          
          <table class="os-raise-skinnytable">
            <tgroup cols="3"><tbody><row>
                <entry><emphasis>\(x\)</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>\(4^x\)</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>\(100x^2\)</emphasis></entry></row>
            <row>
                <entry>\(1\)</entry>
                <entry>\(4\)</entry>
                <entry>\(100\)</entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry>\(2\)</entry>
                <entry>\(16\)</entry>
                <entry>\(400\)</entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry>\(3\)</entry>
                <entry>\(64\)</entry>
                <entry>\(900\)</entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry>\(4\)</entry>
                <entry>\(256\)</entry>
                <entry>\(1,600\)</entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry>\(5\)</entry>
                <entry>\(1,024\)</entry>
                <entry>\(2,500\)</entry>
              </row>
            </tbody></tgroup></table>
          
          <para>Looking at the patterns in the table, choose the best answer to explain why, as the value of \(x\)
            increases, the values of the exponential expression \(4^x\) will eventually overtake the values of the
            quadratic expression \(100x^2\).</para>
       </para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>The value of \(100x^2\) grows by different factors and the value of \(4^x\) grows by a factor of 4 every time.</para><para>The value of \(100x^2\) grows by a factor of 4 every time and the value of \(4^x\) grows by different factors.</para><para>The value of \(100x^2\) grows by different factors and the value of \(4^x\) grows by a factor of \(x\) every time.</para><para>The value of \(100x^2\) grows by a factor of \(x\) every time and the value of \(4^x\) grows by different factors.</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! The value of \(100x^2\) grows by different factors, and the value of \(4^x\) grows by a factor of 4
        every time.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.4.4.</para><para>The correct answer is: The value of \(100x^2\) grows by different factors, and the value of \(4^x\) grows by a
        factor of 4 every time.
     </para></solution></exercise></section></section>
</content>
